[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/stug_iii_cd]
The Wehrmacht High Command issued requirements for the development of an assault vehicle with a 75-mm gun in June 1936. The Daimler-Benz design, derived from the Zugfuhrerwagen tank, designed by the same company and better known as Pz.Kpfw.III, suited the requirements best. The initial plan was to design a combat vehicle with an open top cockpit, but in the end the assault vehicle became fully roofed. The prototype batch of assault vehicles was made in 1937, and the Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.B chassis was used as the basis. 
The first mass-produced model of the assault gun was issued in February 1940. The vehicle was designated Gepanzerte Selbstfahrlafette fur Sturmgeschutz 7.5 cm Kanone Ausf.A, but later the name was reduced to Sturmgeschutz III Ausf.A, or StuG III Ausf.A. The Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.F. tank chassis was used as the basis. Vehicles of this type were employed during the French campaign of May-June 1940. The StuG III Ausf.B was the next modification, produced in much higher numbers, as 320 units of this vehicle were manufactured. The modified Pz.Kpfw.III Ausf.H tank chassis was used as the basis. In addition to the basis, other differences to the StuG III Ausf.A included a new crew compartment. During the production period, the design of the StuG.III Ausf.B underwent several changes, as vehicles of later series received tracks as wide as 400 mm for example. 
A new modification named StuG III Ausf.C was put into mass production in April 1941. As compared to the previous model, the cockpit was redesigned, mainly by moving the sight moved from the front to the top. Ramped plates on cockpit sides were also changed, and the running gear got new track adjusting wheels. A total of 50 StuG III Ausf.C units and 150 StuG III Ausf.D units, which had practically no differences from the previous modification, were manufactured. 
It is worth noting that the main purpose of these assault vehicles was very similar to that of the Pz.Kpfw.IV tank, and the two used the same 75-mm StuK 37 L/24 guns. The point of producing the StuG III therefore was questioned, but the campaign of 1939 vividly demonstrated that infantry units needed a fire support vehicle. In addition, the StuG III was cheaper to make than the Pz.Kpfw.IV. In general, the vehicles of the StuG III family turned out to be the most successful and most produced self-propelled artillery systems at the beginning of World War II.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/stug_III_e]
StuG III Ausf. Е self-propelled gun was designed to replace dedicated SdKfz 253 artillery spotter vehicle, which was removed from StuG regiments.
Additional side 9mm armor sheets were removed to install the armored box on right side and to increase the size of left side armored box containing the radio equipment required for commander vehicle. Order 101 (20 December 1941) obliged crews to carry spare track part 11 links long (on front armor holders). German SPG crews usually placed track parts everywhere they could on the vehicle to enhance armor protection.
There were also standard StuG III Ausf. E SPGs; they didn’t had two antennas as commander vehicles did.
Total 284 (or 272, judging by some sources) vehicles were produced on Alkett plant from September 1941 to March 1942.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/stug_III_f]
After beginning of Barbarossa campaign German self-propelled guns were no longer adequate for supporting infantry. Short-barreled StuK 37 L/24 gun had good ballistics but it was not suited for use against Soviet T-34 and KV tanks, armor of the StuG became inadequate too. Because of this, at 28 September 1941 Hitler ordered firepower upgrade for tanks and self-propelled guns as soon as possible.
StuG III was built on Pz III chassis, but it has the same armament as Pz IV. During December 1941 Rheinmetall-Borsig presented new long-barreled 7.5 cm KwK 40, L/44 gun for new Pz IV Ausf. F. This gun was adapted for StuG III and designated 7.5 cm StuK 40, L/43 (recoil compensation cylinders were moved under the gun to minimize its width and opening in the hull, while there were two such cylinders on sides of the tank gun variant). Gun sight Sfl ZF 1а was used for aiming (through the hatch on the top of the hull). Ammunition load increased (54 shots instead of 44), gun mask was 30mm thick. Sometimes this SPG was called Sturmgeschütz 40 Ausf. F (SdKfz 142/1).
StuG III Ausf. F, unlike its predecessors, could engage Soviet T-34 tanks at large distances. Heavy KV tanks could be knocked out from ~800m, while old StuG guns couldn’t pierce KV front armor even in case of a point-blank shot.
366 StuG III Ausf. F self-propelled guns were produced by Altmaerkische Kettenwerk, GmbH from March to September 1942.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/stug_40_f8]
As the StuG III was designed to fill an infantry close support combat role, early models were fitted with a low-velocity 75 mm StuK 37 L/24 gun to destroy soft-skin targets and fortifications. After the Germans encountered the Soviet KV-1 and T-34 tanks, the StuG III was equipped with a high-velocity 75 mm StuK 40 L/43 main gun (Spring 1942) and later - the 75 mm StuK 40 L/48 (Autumn 1942) anti-tank gun. These versions were known as the Sturmgeschutz 40 Ausfuhrung F, Ausf. F/8 and Ausf. G

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/Hummel]
15cm Schwere Panzerhaubitze auf Geschützwagen III/IV (sf) (15cm Heavy Self-propelled Howitzer "Hummel")

The trials of war showed that the tank and mechanized units required powerful SP howitzers that could provide fast-moving troops with fire support. The requirements for this kind of SP guns were drawn up by 1942, and they were quite ambitious. The proposal was to create full-circle fire SP guns, but the design would require considerable time, and as a temporary solution it was decided to create simpler SP guns with 10.5cm and 15cm howitzers, which became known, respectively, as the Wespe and Hummel (the Wasp and Bumble Bee). In fact, the temporary solution turned to be the only one implemented. Design of the heavy SP gun began in the spring of 1942. The model initially used Pz.IV tank chassis, but later adopted a special Geschützwagen III/IV chassis, a combination of Pz.III and Pz.IV tanks chassis elements. In October 1942 an experimental model of the SP gun, made of machine steel, was presented to the Führer, and serial production of the vehicles began in the spring of 1943. 

Essentially, the Hummel was a light armoured turret, mounted on Geschützwagen III/IV chassis. A 15cm heavy howitzer sFH 18 was mounted in the turret. The SPG, unlike the Soviet SU-152 and ISU-152, was not meant for action at the frontline. As its intended operation was indirect fire, its armour provided only ballistic protection.  However, it was capable of dealing a powerful hollow charge, in case enemy tanks broke through to the gun position. Organizationally, the Hummel was used in the artillery regiments of tank and panzergrenadier divisions, and composed a single battery. According to German classification, self-propelled howitzers were not armoured vehicles, but guns featuring self-propelled carriage. On the whole, the model was a good and reliable SPG.

Hummels were first used in the Battle of Kursk. On July 4, 1943 there were thirty-six such SP guns in Army Group South, evenly distributed across six divisions: in the Panzergrenadier Division Grossdeutschland, the Leibstandarte Adolf Hitler, Das Reich, the Totenkopf Waffen-SS divisions, the 6th and 11th Tank Divisions. Later, Hummels were widely used on all fronts until the end of the war.

A total of 666 SP guns were produced by Alkett and Deutsche Eisenwerke until the termination of serial production in 1944. Also produced were 157 ammunition vehicles that were essentially Hummels with dismounted ordnance. Some of the 10.5mm howitzers were instead mounted on captured French tanks chassis.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/marder_II]
The Marder II ("marten" in English) was a German tank destroyer of World War II based on the Panzer II chassis. There were two versions, the first mounted a modified Soviet 7.62 cm gun firing German ammunition, while the other mounted the German 7.5 cm Pak 40 gun. Its high profile and thin open-topped armor provided minimal protection to the crew. Nevertheless, the Marder II (and similar Marder III) provided a great increase in firepower over contemporary German tanks during 1942 and into 1943. Only four Marder IIs remain today.

The Marder II came in two major versions. The first version (Sd.Kfz. 132) was based on the light Panzer II Ausf. D/E and Flammpanzer II chassis with a new torsion bar suspension featuring four large road wheels and a "slack track" with no track return rollers. It was armed with captured Soviet 7.62 cm guns that were rebuilt to accept the larger German 7.5 cm Pak 40 propellant cartridge. This improved its penetrative capabilities and eliminated the need for captured ammunition. These early Marder IIs had a high silhouette (2.60 m high) and relatively thin armor compared to other armored vehicles; only 30 mm (front) and 10 to 15 mm (sides). There was no armour on the top or rear, leaving the crew with very little protection. Alkett built 150 Marder II (Sd. Kfz. 132) in April/May 1942 and Wegmann converted further 52 from mid 1942 to 1943.

The second version (Sd.Kfz. 131) was based on new-built Panzer II Ausf. F hulls. This Marder II had a redesigned (widened) fighting compartment and used the German 75 mm Pak 40 anti-tank gun. The silhouette was lowered by about 40 cm to 2.20 m, but the armor was thin and the compartment was open to the top and rear, as in Sd. Kfz. 132. Famo (Breslau), and FAMO-Ursus produced 531 Marder II (Sd.Kfz. 131) from July 1942 to June 1943. About 130 more were converted from mid 1943 to early 1944 when the last Panzer IIs were taken out of active service.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/Marder_IIIH_Kursk1943]
7,5cm PaK 40/3 auf Panzerkampfwagen 38(t) Ausf.H (Tank Destroyer Marder IIIH)

By the end of 1941, German design began on a self-propelled artillery mount with modern Pak 40 75mm gun, which could effectively destroy Soviet T-34s and KV tanks. This design made use of the chassis of the Pz.II and Pz.38 (t) light tanks, which were rapidly becoming obsolete, as well as some captured French vehicles. The first prototype of the tank destroyer, which was based on Pz.38(t) chassis, was evidently designed in a hurry, for the vehicle was armed with a refitted captured Soviet F-22 76mm gun, which looked rather awkward, but by April of 1942 the first SP guns were being sent to the frontline. The BMM's designers learned the lessons of their predecessors while working on the next SP gun.

The Marder IIIH was a tank destroyer, created by mounting a light armoured turret with a Pak 40 75mm antitank gun on a Pz.38(t) Ausf.G light tank. The hull of the original tank had hardly been modified – it retained its front plate armour and forward machine gun. The armoured turret mounted in place of the original turret was open at the top and back. The SP gun was quite successful: it was mobile, reliable, and most importantly it could destroy Soviet T-34 and KV tanks. Its main drawback was its light armour, which provided mainly ballistic protection; thus, SP guns avoided exchanging fire with tanks. Their best tactic was a sudden ambush fire and a hasty retreat from their revealed firing position. 
 
The Marder IIIH SP guns were delivered to infantry, panzergrenadier and tank divisions of Wehrmacht and SS, and the independent antitank divisions as well. The peak of their application was in 1943, although some of these vehicles were used further towards the end of the war. On July 4, 1943, there were 20 Marders (primarily the Marder IIIH) in the Panzergrenadier Division Grossdeutschland. SP guns of this type comprised the panzergrenadier divisions of the 2nd SS Tank Corps as well.

The SP gun (also designated as the SdKfz 138) was in production from November 1942 until April 1943, during which time a total of 243 vehicles were produced. In addition, over the year of 1943, one-hundred seventy-five Pz.38(t) tanks were dispatched from the front lines for repair, and were converted into this new type of SP guns. The Pz.38 (t) Marder IIIH was replaced by the Marder IIIM modification, which differed in several major ways from the former model.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/Stug_40_G]
7.5 Sturmgeschütz 40 Ausf.G. Assault Gun (StuG IIIG)

Beginning in the spring of 1942, StuG III assault guns became more and more oriented towards antitank actions. The first modifications of the StuG III with 75mm long-barrel guns were the StuG III Ausf F and the StuG III Ausf F/8. The final and largest production run of the StuG III Ausf G began in December 1942. The main differences of the new vehicle were the slightly altered turret shell, the commander's pod, and a retractable MG shield on the top of the turret. The vehicle was well armoured: the front plate was 80mm thick, first via an additional 30mm armour plate which was bolted on top of original, and later via a one-piece thick plate mounted at the plant. Over the course of serial production, the vehicle design changed several times: armour panels, zimmerit coating, coaxial machine guns and a close-range grenade launcher were introduced, and the shape of the gun mantlet was changed (to so-called Saukopf mantlet); later modifications included a remotely controlled machine gun mounted on top of the turret.

The StuG III Ausf G was a very successful vehicle, and this was reflected in the number of units built – a total of 7,720 vehicles left the assembly line before March 1945, which is vastly greater than any other German tank or SP gun. The StuG IIIG was an excellent combination of fire power (its shells easily penetrated most of the common Soviet and Allied tanks), protection, and production cost. For 1943, the SPG’s front armour plate was quite adequate, as Soviet 45mm and 76mm antitank guns either could not penetrate it at all, or penetrated it only when hit squarely – and even this was only possible with certain projectiles fired from close range. The vehicle's low-to-the-ground form factor provided additional protection. Its rather weak side plating was a drawback, though this was a shortcoming of all medium tanks and SP guns of that time.

The StuG III Ausf G was very widely used in the battles of 1943 through 1945 on all fronts: in Italy, France, and on the Eastern Front. StuG III guns were provided to both tank and panzergrenadier divisions, which by the end the war frequently replaced tanks, while special units received SP gun divisions. By July of 1943 there were 34 StuG III guns in the Panzergrenadier Division Grossdeutschland, and by July 31 of the same year only one of those vehicles was decommissioned as an irrecoverable loss. By that time, there had been a total of only twenty-two irrecoverable losses of StuG IIIs by the whole of Army Group South.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/Stug_40_G_Kursk1943]
7.5 Sturmgeschütz 40 Ausf.G. Assault Gun (StuG IIIG) (early)

Beginning in the spring of 1942, StuG III assault guns became more and more oriented towards antitank actions. The first modifications of the StuG III with 75mm long-barrel guns were the StuG III Ausf F and the StuG III Ausf F/8. The final and largest production run of the StuG III Ausf G began in December 1942. The main differences of the new vehicle were the slightly altered turret shell, the commander's pod, and a retractable MG shield on the top of the turret. The vehicle was well armoured: the front plate was 80mm thick, first via an additional 30mm armour plate which was bolted on top of original, and later via a one-piece thick plate mounted at the plant. Over the course of serial production, the vehicle design changed several times: armour panels, zimmerit coating, coaxial machine guns and a close-range grenade launcher were introduced, and the shape of the gun mantlet was changed (to so-called Saukopf mantlet); later modifications included a remotely controlled machine gun mounted on top of the turret.

The StuG III Ausf G was a very successful vehicle, and this was reflected in the number of units built – a total of 7,720 vehicles left the assembly line before March 1945, which is vastly greater than any other German tank or SP gun. The StuG IIIG was an excellent combination of fire power (its shells easily penetrated most of the common Soviet and Allied tanks), protection, and production cost. For 1943, the SPG’s front armour plate was quite adequate, as Soviet 45mm and 76mm antitank guns either could not penetrate it at all, or penetrated it only when hit squarely – and even this was only possible with certain projectiles fired from close range. The vehicle's low-to-the-ground form factor provided additional protection. Its rather weak side plating was a drawback, though this was a shortcoming of all medium tanks and SP guns of that time.

The StuG III Ausf G was very widely used in the battles of 1943 through 1945 on all fronts: in Italy, France, and on the Eastern Front. StuG III guns were provided to both tank and panzergrenadier divisions, which by the end the war frequently replaced tanks, while special units received SP gun divisions. By July of 1943 there were 34 StuG III guns in the Panzergrenadier Division Grossdeutschland, and by July 31 of the same year only one of those vehicles was decommissioned as an irrecoverable loss. By that time, there had been a total of only twenty-two irrecoverable losses of StuG IIIs by the whole of Army Group South.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/stug_iv]
The development of the Sturmgeschutz IV tank destroyer was the result of an accident. Allied aviation destroyed a plant of the Alkett company in November 1943. The plant had produced StuG 40 Ausf.G tank destroyers, and its destruction put continuing supply of self-propelled gun systems of that type at a risk. A solution to the problem was suggested by the Krupp company, which came up with a design designated Krupp No W 1468. The design envisioned mounting the cockpit of the StuG 40 Ausf.G on a Pz.Kpfw.IV tank chassis. The vehicle was put into production in December 1943 under the designation of Sturmgeschutz IV with an index of Sd.Kfz.167. As compared to StuG 40 Ausf.G, the position of the driver changed a bit. He received a separate cockpit with a top boarding hatch . A total of 1,139 StuG IV units were manufactured. The vehicles of later series were different in using the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.J tank as the chassis, and their running gear and exhaust system were a bit different, too.
Even though the StuG IV was created in an extremely short time, the vehicle turned out to be quite successful. As compared to the StuG 40 Ausf.G, travelling comfort was somewhat poorer, but on the other hand, the ammunition storage space increased. In the end, the StuG IV was quite suitable for the tank destroyer role. 

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/stug_40_g_sk]
In 1944 StuG 40 Ausf.G SPGs with new streamlined gun mantle started to arrive to German forces. This mantle was called "Saukopfblende" (pig snout) for its distinctive lines. SPG was also armed with two machine guns – one in gun mantle and another on the roof.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/stuh_42]
Initially StuG III was designed as direct fire support assault gun for infantry and the armament of StuG III Ausf. A-E SPGs – short-barelled 7.5cm Stu.K. 37 L/24 tank gun – was intended for eliminating enemy infantry in trenches, HMG and artillery positions, light pillboxes. But when Blitzkrieg against USSR turned out to be a long and exhaustive war, German high command was forced to revise StuG tactics to combat Soviet T-34 and KV tanks. Starting in March 1942, production of new StuGs with long-barreled guns intended for anti-tank use begun, and almost all early StuGs were rearmed with long-barreled guns before Summer 1943. As a compensation, company Altmaerkische Kettenwerk, GmbH was ordered to produce in 1942 12 SPGs with 10.5cm le F.H.  howitzers made by Rheinmetall-Borsig.
New cannon differed from field howitzer by recoil-dampener, two-chambered muzzle brake and breech mechanism, while barrel and ballistics remained the same. Assault howitzer could be turned in 24° angle horizontally and from -10° to +20° vertically (much lower than optimal for howitzer 45°). Ammunition reserve consisted of 36 rounds (shell and round itself were separated before loading) – Sprenggranaten (HE) и Hohlladungsgranaten (AP). Additional weaponry consisted of MG-34.
First experimental howitzer was installed in StuG III Ausf.E by Alkett before March 1942, and at 2nd October 1942 Albert Shpeer showed it to Hitler and said that 6 Sturmhaubitze of 12 planned are ready. Fuhrer was very pleased with new vehicle. Experimental series was made of early StuG III SPGs, while StuG III Ausf.F/8, which were produced in that time, were not involved in the experiment. Mass production of Sturmhaubitze begun at March 1943.
18 March 1943 new vehicle was officially designated Sturmgeschütz III für 10.5 cm Stu.Haub.42 (Sd.Kfz.142/2), but in August 1944 Generalinspektuer der Panzertroppen called it more shortly – Sturmhaubitze 42 (StuH 42), (SdKfz 142/2). This SPGs were made using StuG III Ausf.F (prototypes), Ausf.F/8 и Ausf.G, almost all changes in StuG III construction were effective for StuH 42 too.
In September 1944 StuH 42 were produced without muzzle brakes (muzzle brakes from le.FH.18 and le.18/40 were planned to be used instead).
In the middle of 1944 StuH 42 received new cast gun mantle Tpfblende, but SPGs with welded mantles were produced in parallel.
StuH 42 SPGs were issued to SPG battalions in motorized divisions and SPG battalions in infantry divisions. Near the end of the war sometimes AT battalions in Panzer divisions had company of StuH 42 instead of Stug III, Germans tried to keep the proportion of 7 StuG III : 3 StuH 42 in a battery.
These assault howitzers saw first-time action in Kursk salient (68 units were deployed). After that, they were used in Africa, Italy and France as well as East front.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/Wespe]
Leichte Feldhaubitze 18/2 auf Fahrgestell Panzerkampfwagen II (Sf) (10.5cm Light Self-propelled Howitzer Wespe)

Experience on the field showed that tank and vehicle units required SP artillery that could provide fire support for fast-moving troops and quickly engage revealed targets. German industry managed to supply its forces with all types of SP vehicles, from assault and antitank SP vehicles, to SP howitzers.

In the spring of 1942, design began on a self-propelled vehicle with a 10.5cm light field howitzer. A Pz.II light tank chassis was chosen for the base, as it was very easy to manufacture, though it needed to be significantly modified. After trials and further adaptation, the new SPG was accepted for service, and its serial production began in February 1943.

Essentially, the SP vehicle was a light armoured turret, mounted on a redesigned Pz.II tank chassis. A 10.5cm howitzer leFH 18 was mounted in the turret. It must be noted, that the Wespe ("Wasp" – the name that this SP gun received in 1943), unlike the Soviet SU-122 (which was essentially an assault gun) was an SP howitzer through and through, and therefore it was not suited for actions on the front line. Its main application was firing from covered positions, as so its armour provided protection only from bullets. Nevertheless, it had some means of defending itself against heavier threats: the SP gun's ammunition included armour-piercing and hollow charges that could knock out any encroaching enemy tank. German SP howitzers differed from the Soviet's organizationally – instead of separate SP artillery regiments, as they were arranged in the USSR, the German SP guns belonged to artillery regiments of the panzergrenadier and tank divisions, making up one of its squadrons. By the German classification, SP howitzers were not armour vehicles, but were instead considered guns featuring a self-propelled carriage.

The Wespe's entered the war for the first time at the Battle of Kursk. On July 4, 1943 there were fifty-four such SP guns in Army Group South. They were in the Panzergrenadier Division Grossdeutschland, the Leibstandarte Adolf Hitler, Das Reich, and the Totenkopf Waffen-SS divisions. And there were six more vehicles in 7th Tank Division as well. After this, the Wespe was widely used on all fronts until the end of the war.

A total of 675 SP guns were produced by FAMO Company until the termination of serial production in July 1944. Additionally, FAMO built 159 ammunition vehicles that represented the Wespe minus the ordnance. In addition, some of these 10.5mm howitzers were mounted on captured French tanks chassis.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/ferdinand]
This is theWehrmacht’s most famous self-propelled antitank system. It is derived from the Tiger tank, designed by the Porsche company. Judging by military memoirs, several thousand Ferdinand self-propelled artillery vehicles were destroyed on the Eastern Front alone. Meanwhile, the Alkett company manufactured only 90 vehicles of this type in 1943. The chassis is borrowed from the VK4501(P) experimental tank. The fully armored cockpit was located in the back of the vehicle. The front section of the vehicle housed the driver and the radio operator, and the power plant was positioned behind them. In late 1943, a bow gun was mounted on the front section of 48 Ferdinand vehicles. A command tower appeared on top of the cockpit. The Ferdinands were baptized by fire in the Battle of Kursk as part of the 653rd and 654th tank destroyer battalions. Each had 45 vehicles. The battalions destroyed a total of 556 Soviet tanks from July to November 1943. 
All Ferdinand vehicles, which had been renamed Elefant by then, were recalled from the Eastern front in late 1943.
After an upgrade, all vehicles were added to the 653rd tank destroyer battalion and fought in Italy. In winter 1945, Elefants took part in the German counterattack in the Ardennes. As many as 28 combat vehicles of this type were still in the inventory in late March.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/elefant]
8.8 cm PaK 43/2 Sfl L/71 Panzerjäger Tiger (P) (tank destroyer Elefant on the base of Tiger)

The Elefant heavy tank destroyer, a modification of the Ferdinand SPG, was intended to improve gun's combat and operational performance, based on field experience. The upgrade of the SPG was carried out in late 1943 and early 1944 at the Nibelungenwerk factory. All 47 upgraded Ferdinands would be attached to the 653rd heavy anti-tank battalion.

The upgrade included the following: a machine gun mounted onto the SPG's frontal armour, replacement gun barrels, the protective barrel cover rotated by 180 degrees to improve the mount, a commander's cupola with seven fixed periscopes, swapped lighting generator polarity, improved sealing of exhaust manifolds, 30mm armour plate added to the bottom front armour for improved protection against mines, wider tracks, ammo capacity increased by 5 shells, and body tool and track mounts. In addition, the body and turret were coated with Zimmerit paste.

In January 1944, a company of SPGs (consisting of 14 units) was deployed in Italy to oppose the advancing Allied forces. By the beginning of June 1944, he company had seen combat at Nettuno, Anzio, and Rome, and established a fine reputation.

In April of 1944, the remaining two companies were redeployed in Galicia along the Eastern Front, to take part in fierce combat against the advancing Soviet forces. In August 1944, the heavily tattered 653rd battalion was recalled to Germany and re-equipped with Jagdtigers. The remaining 13 Elefants were combined into the 614th heavy anti-tank company. They saw their final service in Wünsdorf and the suburbs of Berlin.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/jagdpanther]
Panzerjäger V Jagdpanther (tank destroyer Jagdpanther)

The development of a new SPG was initiated in August 1942 during the prototype testing of the Pz.Kpfw.V Panther. The assault tank was to be equipped with an 88mm main gun, and a 7.92mm machine gun for defence. After numerous re-evaluations, full-scale development was launched in May of 1943, and the final armaments draft was approved in October. That same month, the dummy gun, destined to become a tank destroyer, was presented to Hitler, with a second prototype demonstrated in December.

The final modification, designated Panzerjager fuer 8.8cm PaK 43 auf Fgst Panther I (Sd.Kfz.173) was equipped with a 88mm Pak 43/2 L/71 gun and a 7.92mm machine gun, in a ball mount similar to that of the Pz.Kpfw.V Ausf.A and G. This tank destroyer was better known as the Jagdpanzer V Jagdpanther, or simply the Jagdpanther. During mass production, launched in January of 1944, the Jagdpanther was modified almost monthly. The manufactured SPGs can be divided into the three main series. The first was equipped with a Pak 43/2 gun with a solid gun barrel and a gun mask welded to front armour. The second series, launched in the summer of 1944, had a bolted gun mask and a Pak 43/2 with a two-part barrel. The third series, launched late in 1944, featured modified mufflers, and the engine deck of the later Pz.Kpfw.V Ausf.G with an additional fan. In addition, numerous minor modifications were introduced, like the frequently altered driver's visor. About four hundred Jagdpanthers were assembled.

The Jagdpanther is rightfully considered one of the best anti-tank SPGs of World War II. Its firepower matched the Ferdinand/Elefant SPG – or the Pz.Kpfw.VI Ausf.B Tiger II heavy tank – but the Jagdpanther was more mobile. Besides, unlike its Soviet counterparts, the Jagdpanther had a more comfortable crew compartment which also improved its overall efficiency.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/Brummbar]
Sturmpanzer IV Brummbär

In 1939, German engineers began work on an SPG armed with a 150mm s.I.G. 33 howitzer, and in 1940-1942, they assembled small numbers of military vehicles based on the Pz.Kpfw.I, Pz.Kpfw.II and Pz.Kpw.III tanks. The latest of these, the SturmInfanterieGeschutz 33B (StuIG 33B), became the prototype of a mass-produced SPG based on the Pz.Kpfw.IV tank. Like its predecessor, this vehicle's key feature was a spacious, well-defended cupola instead of a turret. It was armed with a radically modified sIG 33 howitzer, renamed the 15cm StuH 43 L/12. Unlike its prototype, the new model was ball-mounted, and the gun’s dimensions and recoil were also greatly reduced.

Development of an assault gun, named the StuG IV mit 15 cm StuH 43, was launched in October of 1942, with the first units mass-produced in April 1943. This SPG was better known as the Sturmpanzer IV Brummbär (Sd.Kfz.166). Later vehicles had a modified driver's compartment, as well as a ball-mounted 7.92mm MG-34 machine gun. In total, 306 of these SPGs were produced.

The Sturmpanzer Brummbär was a shining success. Well-armoured and boasting decent firepower, it could fight well against infantry and fortifications, as well as tanks. Its hollow-charge shell could penetrate 160mm armour plate, which meant that the Brummbärs was a threat to all tanks on the field. Its few drawbacks included a small ammo capacity, and a somewhat cramped fighting compartment.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/jagdpanzer_38t_(hetzer)]
Jagdpanzer 38(t)

The history of the Hetzer light tank destroyer began in March 1943, when Colonel General Heinz Guderian ordered the creation of a tank destroyer based on the Pz.Kpfw 38(t) tank. It was to be fully armoured and low-profile, and equipped with a gun capable of engaging enemy tanks at ranges in excess of 800 meters.

The Hetzer was produced by the famous CzKD in Prague, renamed Boehmisch-Maehrishe-Maschinenfabrik (BMM) during the occupation. The company had completed the design by December 17, 1943, and an early prototype was produced in January 24, 1944. In March/April, three prototypes were built for demonstration and approval by Hitler on the proving grounds in Arys, East Prussia. The new tank destroyer was then adopted by the German army as the Sd.Kfz.182 Jagdpanzer 38 (t) Hetzer.

After mass production commenced on the Jagdpanzer 38 (t) Hetzer, it became obvious that ВММ alone could not handle the first order for 1,000 units, so the Skoda factory, in Pilsen, was included in the manufacturing line. The gun itself was produced by Rheinmetall-Borsig and Seitz Werke in Germany. The production process had been refined and streamlined by mid-1944. About 1,570 units were made during the war.

Hetzers were widely used in the battles for East Prussia, Pomerania, and Silesia, as well as the German Ardennes offensive. Thanks to its armour slope and low profile, the Jagdpanzer 38 (t) Hetzer was a great example of an anti-tank gun capable of ambushing and quick redeployment. However, its gun – a 75mm Pak 39/L 48 – was clearly inferior to the guns of the T-34-85 and IS, which meant that the Jagdpanzer 38 (t) Hetzer was inefficient in artillery duels at long ranges. This shortcoming aside, the Jagdpanzer 38 (t) Hetzer was a perfect close combat weapon.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/nashorn]
The first self-propelled artillery vehicle/tank destroyer was derived from the Geschuetzwagen Ut/IV (GW III/IV). The latter was a specialized chassis bringing together components of the Pz.III and Pz.IV tanks. The vehicle was designed by the Deutsche Eisenwerke company. As many as 494 vehicles were made in 1943-1945.
The running gear and the lower body section were taken from the Pz.IV tank, and the engine and transmission from the Pz.III. The HL 120 TRM engine was positioned behind the transmission and formed a single block together with it. The spacious combat cockpit with an 88-mm antitank gun was positioned in the rear of the body and opened on top and partially in the back. The machine-gun was not mounted permanently and was intended for self-defense. The system was originally named Hornisse, but was given the name of Nashorn in January 1944 following Hitler’s personal instruction. These vehicles were in the inventory of the High Command reserve antitank battalions and took part in combat operations until the end of the war. As of April 10, 1945, the Wehrmacht possessed 62 systems of this type on the Eastern Front and 23 systems on the Western front. 

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/jagdpanzer_iv]
In early 1943, the Vomag company started developing an assault gun that was supposed to replace the StuG 40 Ausf.G in the future. The wooden mock-up was ready in May 1943, and Vomag manufactured the first prototype in October. The weapon that was being developed as an assault gun in fact turned out a tank destroyer dramatically different from previous German vehicles of this type. Derived from the Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.F tank, the vehicle has a very low silhuette, which provided a strong advantage in combat. Its main armament was the 75-mm Pak 39 L/43 gun equipped with a two-chamber recoil compensator. Vehicles of Series Zero were distinguished by rounded corners of the cockpit nose. Mass-produced tank destroyers featured a simplified body design and a new, more powerful 75-mm gun, Pak 39 L/48.
Mass-production began in January 1944. The vehicle got the official designation of Sturmgeschuetz neuer Art mit 7.5cm PaK L/48 auf Fahrgestell Panzerkampfwagen IV, which was later changed to Jagdpanzer IV Ausf.F. It also had the index of Sd.Kfz.162. A total of 769 Jagdpanzer IV Ausf.F units were manufactured, after which they were completely replaced by Panzer IV70 (V) self-propelled artillery vehicles. The vehicles underwent some changes during the production period, in particular, vehicles of later series did not have recoil compensators, which crews often removed anyway in order to reduce signature while firing. 
The main advantage of Jagdpanzer IV Ausf.F was a low silhuette in combination with a powerful gun. The front armor plane as thick as 60 mm was not a sufficient protection against enemy tank fire, but this was compensated by the low signature of the vehicle. In general, Vomag designers created a good tank destroyer. However, a lighter and more maneuverable self-propelled artillery vehicle, the Jagdpanzer 38(t) Hetzer, which had the same gun, was put into mass production already in spring. 

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/jagdpanzer_iv_70]
While developing the Jagdpanzer IV self-propelled artillery vehicle, designers considered the possibility of equipping it with the 75-mm Kw.K 42 L/70 gun. The same gun was installed in Pz.Kpfw.V Panther tanks. Right after the beginning of the mass-production of the Jagdpanzer IV Ausf.F, work on equipping it with a new gun started anew and was completed in April 1944. The resulting vehicle was a redesigned model of the Jagdpanzer IV Ausf.F with a reinforced running gear and front armor plate thickness increased to 80 mm. The 75-mm StuK 42 L/70 gun, a derivative of the Kw.K 42 L/70 gun, was used as the main weapon.
Mass production of the vehicle, which was designated Panzer IV/70(V) and given the index of Sd.Kfz.162/1, began in August 1944. The vehicles were manufactured in three series, each featuring minor changes. A total of 930 Panzer IV/70(V) units were made.
As compared to its predecessor, the Panzer IV/70(V) was distinguished by stronger armor protection and more powerful armament capable of engaging enemy tanks at long distances. On the other hand, problems with unequal distribution of load on suspension wheels, from which the Jagdpanzer IV Ausf.F suffered, got even worse for the new vehicle. The Panzer IV/70(e) project with new front suspension wheels was developed, but never put into mass-production. 

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/ger/jagdtiger]
In early 1943 the decision was made to derive a tank destroyer from the Tiger II tank and arm it with the 128-mm Рак 44 semi-automatic anti-aircraft gun. The wooden mock-up of the huge vehicle was demonstrated already on October 20 that year, and the prototype was shown in April 1944. The production was slowed down a bit as the running gear designed by the Porsche company did not turn out a success, and a new running gear with lateral torsion bars was developed. The initial plan was to manufacture 150 vehicles, but the Niebelungen company only made 70 Jagdtigers, of which only 48 remained among the ranks at the end of the war. 
The first battalion of Jagdtiger heavy antitank self-propelled artillery vehicles was established in summer 1944 and given the number of 512. It was baptized by fire near the river Rhein in March 1945. Jagdtigers managed to hit U.S. tanks from a distance of over 2,500 m in that battle. 
The 653rd tank destroyer battalion, to which Jagdtigers were added as well, took part in the offensive in the Ardennes and later teamed up with the 512th battalion to conduct defensive battles on German soil in March 1945. 

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/Su_12m_Kursk1943]
SU-76 (SU-12M) Light Self-propelled Gun

In March of 1942, a light self-propelled gun mounted on a T-60 tank chassis was drawn up in the design bureau under the supervision of S.A. Ginsburg. In May of that year, a prototype SP gun was tested, but it was not put into serial production. Further development of a light SP gun was done on the base of the T-70 tank chassis. In October of 1942, the Soviet State Defence Committee ordered the development of assault SP guns, and two plants joined the project: GAZ (which designated the project SU-71) and Plant No. 38 (project SU-12). By November of 1942, vehicles from both plants were tested, and the SU-12 prevailed, and it was accepted for service in the Red Army in December 1942 as the SU-76. Serial production of this SP gun began immediately Plant No. 38, and by January 1, 1943 the first batch of 25 vehicles emerged from the assembly line.

The feature of the SP gun was the installation of two concurrent GAZ-202 engines powering the same shaft. By the end of January, two SP gun regiments were dispatched to the Volkhov Front. Field applications showed that the two engines were not fully synchronized, and the resulting resonance oscillations quickly destroyed the shaft line. On March 21, 1943, after a total of 350 vehicles had been produced, production of the SP guns was frozen. Urgent adaption of SP guns was required, and additional coil clutches were inserted between the engine and transmission; some other minor improvements were performed as well. In May, the serial production of new vehicles, designated SU-12M, resumed. The SU-12 guns produced earlier were upgraded to SU-12M design as well. In July of 1943, the serial production of the SU-76 was terminated because a new, more modern SP gun, the SU-76M (SU-15), was accepted for service. In the interim, a total of 560 vehicles were produced.

Technically, the SU-76 was based on the T-70 tank. Its main armament was a 76mm ZiS3-Sh gun, mounted in light armour and a fully closed turret, although after the Battle of Kursk the roof of the turret was sometimes removed to improve the SPG’s airing. The armour protected the SPG from small arms, heavy machine guns, small-calibre automatic guns, shells, and bomb fragments. Tactically these SP guns were used for infantry fire support; they moved behind the infantry and engaged discovered targets – machine guns, mortars, and regimental and AA positions. An attempt at using SP guns on the front line, as full-blown tanks, led to heavy and inexcusable losses. But if necessary, the SU-76 could engage enemy tanks as well, although the ZiS-3Sh gun was inefficient against the front plates of the newly modified German Pz.III and Pz.IV tanks, not to mention Tigers and Panthers.

Organizationally, the SU-76 was the part of mixed self-propelled artillery regiments (SPARs), and each regiment had eight SU-76 and twelve SU-122, though the first regiments were assigned seventeen SU-76 and eight SU-122. Since April of 1943, new SPAR were formed, each including twenty-one SU-76. Mixed SPAR were engaged in the Battle of Kursk under the command of the Voronezh Front, the 1438th, 1440th, 1450th (as part of 10th Tank Corps), the 1461st and – under command of 5th Guard Tank Army – the 1446th and 1447th SPARs. By July 5th, the SU-76 of the 1440th SPAR engaged the German forces which were assaulting Cherkasskoe village.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/Su_76m]
SU-76M Self-Propelled Gun M1943

By early 1943, the SU-76 (SU-12), a light self-propelled gun mounted on the T-70 light tank, had completed the design phase and entered mass production. However, production was halted in March 1943 due to problems with the power plant's reliability. By May, the critical issues had been resolved, and mass production resumed on the newer SU-12M. However, the military was not entirely pleased with the new SPG variant: there were problems with the synchronization of the vehicle’s two gearboxes; the driver was seated between the engines; the fighting compartment was poorly ventilated; and there were certain reliability issues as well. For these reasons, the decision was made to create a new SPG based on the same chassis, but one which did not share the drawbacks of its predecessor.
The design bureau of Factory No. 38 created three prototypes with different power plant layouts, alternate armor, and so on. All three enter testing in July of 1943, and the SU-15 was found to be the most reliable and mobile, and simpler to manufacture than the SU-76. However, a few improvements were still in order, and a reduction in weight was found necessary. This was achieved by making the fighting cab open-top. Late in August 1943, the new light SPG was adopted and entered mass production, which continued through the end of 1945.
In terms of design, the SU-76M was a light, open-top SPG with fragmentation armor. One interesting peculiarity was that its power plant consisted of two serially coupled engines. The SPG’s primary armament was a special variant of the ZIS-3 divisional gun. In addition to this, the frontal armor had a special port and mount for a DT machine gun. The SPG’s primary task was infantry support – laying down direct fire at machine gun nests, infantry guns, mortars, and earth-and-timber emplacements and buildings. The SU-76M could also deal with armored vehicles as needed, but its gun could not easily penetrate heavily armored tanks and SPGs.
Many SU-76M guns were constructed and saw active use during the second half of the Great Patriotic War. During the postwar years, the SU-76M was further improved, and served in the Soviet Army until the 1960s. The SU-76 also served as the foundation for the small-batch ZSU-37 anti-aircraft SPG.
The SU-76M served in other countries as well. In particular, a few dozen of these guns were exported to North Korea and China, and saw use in the Korean War.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/su_85]
85mm Anti-Tank SPG Model 1943 (SU-85)

In January of 1943, the Soviets captured two Pz.Kpfw.VI Ausf.H1 Tiger heavy tanks. Tests conducted later, in April, indicated that their own 76mm F-34 and ZIS-5 tank guns could only penetrate German armour plates at the close range of 100-200 metres. Their best means against these Tigers was the 85mm 52-K anti-aircraft gun, as well as the 122mm A-19 gun-howitzer. At about the same time, they began developing a few new 85mm tank guns, including the D-5T, the first prototypes of which were produced in June.

Meanwhile, in May 1943, the Soviets also launched development of a tank destroyer based on their SU-122 self-propelled gun. The first two prototypes were equipped with 85mm S-18 guns, but the third prototype produced some time later -- SU-85-II -- was armed with an 85mm D-5S, an SPG modification of the D-5T. Testing proved the superiority of the SU-85-II, and in August of 1943 it entered mass production as the SU-85. A total of 2050 SU-85 self-propelled guns were produced over its production lifetime.

The SU-85 was the Soviet answer to the German StuG 40 Ausf.G self-propelled gun. Its D-5S had decent armour-piercing capabilities for mid-1943, and was able to penetrate a 100mm armour plate at a 1-kilometer range. This was quite enough to destroy such tanks as the Pz.Kpfw.V Panther and the Pz.Kpfw.VI Ausf.H1 Tiger.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/su_85m]
85 mm anti-tank self-propelled gun model 1944 (SU-85M)

While Uralmashzavod was preparing for the production of the SU-100, a project was developed for the SU-85M transition SPG, which was a SU-100 hull with an armament of the 85-mm D-5S-85 cannon mounted on the SU-85. In addition to the gun model, the SU-85M differed from the SU-100 only by a similar SU-85 rotary mechanism, a marching mount and a gun mask, a sight and a 60 85-mm ammunition load. The SU-85M allowed earlier to introduce improvements to the SU-100 — more powerful frontal armor and better surveillance devices - in mass production, but the main reason for the appearance of this previously unplanned SPG was the incapacity at that time of the 100-mm gun, the production of armor-piercing shells B-412B to which was mastered only in November 1944. The first SU-85M was manufactured in July 1944, and in August it completely replaced the SU-85 on the assembly lines of Uralmashzavod. The production of the SU-85M continued until November of the same year, for three months — in parallel with the SU-100, which were not operational at that time due to the lack of armor-piercing shells. A total of 315 self-propelled guns of this type were produced.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/Su_100]
SU-100 Self-Propelled Gun M1944

The SU-85, mounted on the T-34 chassis, entered mass production in 1943 and established a good reputation on the front lines, but the military were not entirely pleased with the design. German 75mm guns remained a severe threat, and the frontal plates of heavily armored German tanks could only be penetrated at close range. The situation called for better armor and more powerful armaments.
The 100mm gun was determined to be the best means of improving the SPG's armaments, and was authorized for production on November 11, 1943. A prototype had been completed by February 1943, but field tests, which also involved two competing prototypes, continued into the summer. Finally, the D-10S gun prevailed, and the SU-100 was adopted on July 3, 1944. However, due to issues with the manufacture of its armor-piercing shells, it only entered mass production that September.
In terms of design, the SU-100 was a development of SU-85, featuring a more powerful gun, 75mm frontal armor, and a commander’s cupola. The excellent ballistic qualities of the 100mm gun meant a renewed threat to enemy German tanks and SPGs, and its HE shells were good against fieldworks. In turn, the frontal armor stood good chances against the widespread German 75mm anti-tank and tank guns. On the downside, the SU-100 had no machine gun, which reduced its anti-personnel capabilities, and its front rollers were overloaded.
The SU-100 SPG fought in the final stages of the Great Patriotic War, and established for itself an excellent reputation. When mass production ended in 1946, these vehicles continued serving the Soviet Army, and were finally upgraded in the late 1950s. The SU-100 did not fight in the Korean War, but if the war had evolved into a full-scale armed conflict, they would certainly have been mobilized.
During the postwar years, numerous SU-100 SPGs were exported across the globe to various allies of the USSR, and in the 1950s, the SU-100 was licensed for production in Czechoslovakia. In some parts of the world, these vehicles saw continued use into the 21st century, and have been actively used in Arab-Israeli conflicts.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/Su_122]
SU-122 Medium Assault Self-propelled Artillery Gun

On October 19, 1942, the State Defence Committee decided to begin the design of a self-propelled artillery gun, equipped with a 122mm M-30 howitzer. Design of the SP gun was handled by the design bureaus of two plants: the UZTM (in Uralmash) and Plant No. 592. The UZTM's design was completed in the summer of 1942. The resulting U-35, which was based on the SPG project U-34, involved the mounting of a 76mm gun on the base of T-34 tank chassis. The design of Plant No. 592 was essentially a 122mm howitzer mounted on a captured Pz.III tank chassis. Work on that project, designated SG-122, began in the spring of 1942. In December the 1942 prototype vehicles were tested, and UZTM's design was chosen for serial production. A small batch of SG-122 guns had already been produced, and in the spring of 1943 were dispatched to the depots. The serial production of the SU-122 began in January 1943 at the UZTM.

The SU-122 was a powerful assault SP gun, designed for the direct support of charging infantry in battle. The powerful howitzer charge enabled effective engagement of enemy firing positions, including hidden and fortified positions. The SP gun also proved very useful in urban combat. It was not the task of the SP gun to engage tanks. Although the vehicle's ammunition included several powerful hollow charges for self-defence, the low-ranged optical sight of the vehicle made it ineffective against tanks in medium- and long-range combat. Using the T-34 chassis simplified maintenance and repair work.
At the same time, the SU-122 had several drawbacks. Its fighting cab was reported to be too cramped, a problem only worsened by the strong gun recoil. The vehicle commander had to carry out the functions of the second gunner, because the directional gear was located on either side of the barrel. The visual ability of this SP gun also left much to be desired, for there was no command pod, and the driver's vision was significantly obstructed by huge armoured case of recoil absorbers. The lack of a mounted machine gun could also be included on the list of shortcomings. Some of these problems were resolved with the modernized SU-122M, which was tested in June, 1943. But despite the successful tests, the SU-122M never entered serial production. It was decided to terminate the production of the SU-122 altogether, in favour of the antitank SU-85 SP gun. After the Battle of Kursk, priority was placed on antitank actions.

Organizationally, the SU-76 was the part of mixed self-propelled artillery regiments (SPARs), and each regiment had eight SU-76 and twelve SU-122, though the first regiments were assigned seventeen SU-76 and eight SU-122. Since April of 1943, new SPAR were formed, each including 16 SU-122. Mixed SPAR were engaged in the Battle of Kursk under the command of the Voronezh Front, the 1438th, 1440th, 1450th (as part of 10th Tank Corps), the 1461st and – under command of 5th Guard Tank Army – the 1446th and 1447th SPARs. By July 5th, the SU-76  of the 1440th SPAR engaged the German forces which were assaulting Cherkasskoe village.

The SU-122 was widely used in 1944 as well, though the number of vehicles in the field continuously decreased. By 1945, few of them saw any action, and on April 15th of 1945 there were only two SU-122 in 4th Guard Tank Army. Five of these SP guns remained in 7th Guard Mechanized Corps, two of which were destroyed by faustpatrones in the Battle of Bautzen.

A total of six-hundred and forty SU-122 SP guns were produced by the UZTM before the line was retired in August of 1943.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/Isu_152]
ISU-152 Heavy Self-Propelled Gun M1944

In the spring of 1943, the SU-152 heavy SPG entered mass production. The essence of the design was the ML-20 152mm howitzer-gun mounted on the KV-1S heavy tank chassis. However, by the summer of 1943, the IS tank was considered the more viable vehicle, and ultimately it would replaced the KV on the conveyor belt. Therefore the decision was made to create a SU-152 counterpart based on the IS chassis. In addition to improving the chassis, this redesign would raise the specifications of the SPG, its armor in particular.
Towards the end of the summer of 1943, the prototype, designated the ISU-152, was presented to Stalin. The SPG was officially adopted on September 4, but it another month to fix certain problems exposed during the field tests. Mass production was launched in November of 1943 and continued into 1946.
The ISU-152 was a very powerful attack SPG. Its 50kg HE shells could destroy any fieldworks, and in urban combat, it only took one or two hits to pull a building down. Heavy armor-piercing shells – as well its HE shells – could penetrate almost any type of enemy armored vehicles. The ISU-152’s own armor provided decent protection from the German 75mm anti-tank and tank guns, which had become by that time a common threat.
On the downside, its rate of fire was low, its ammunition load was small, and its original design lacked a machine gun. However, from 1945 onwards, the ISU-152 was also equipped with the DShK large-caliber anti-aircraft machine gun.
During the postwar years, the ISU-152 SPG enjoyed a long service history with the Soviet Army and was upgraded late in the 1950s with a new engine and a larger ammunition load. Unlike the similar ISU-122, the ISU-152 did not take part in the Korean War, although it was used by the Egyptian forces against the Israelis.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/isu_122]
Success of SU-152 design made development of similar SPGs using IS tank hulls (produced in Chelyabinsk) possible. Development of prototype “Object 241” was carried out simultaneously with IS-85. New SPG looked like SU-152 but was taller (KV hull was more deep than IS hull) to accommodate needed equipment and ammo. Ammunition reserve stayed the same – 20 rounds (shell and round itself were separated before loading). “Object 241” and SU-152 had the same gun – 152mm gun-howitzer ML-20S. First prototype was shown to GKO (State Defense Committee) during Summer 1943 and new SPG was designated ISU-152.
Biggest problem was lack of ML-20 gun barrels – Soviet tank factories could not produce enough. But there were plenty of 122mm A-19 guns and ammunition for them, because of that GKO ordered Chelyabinsk design bureau SKB-2 to find a way to install A-19 to ISU-152 hull. It was relatively easy task – A-19 and ML-20 were using the same gun carriage for using in the field, so only ammunition rack was re-designed. Experimental prototype “Object 242” was ready before end of 1943 and new SPG was designated ISU-122. First 35 ISU-152 and ISU-122 SPGs were made in December 1943 along with IS-85 production.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/rus/isu_122s]
Soviet engineers tried to increase ISU firepower during the war. A-19 cannon, modernized for use in IS-2 tanks, had ‘falling’ breech-mechanism. New D-25S cannon had rate of fire of 2-3 shots per minute (A-19 had ROF of 1.5-2 spm). Soon it was decided to install the new cannon on ISU-122 too, new prototype was called “Object 249”, it had new gun mantle with better horizontal fire angles in addition to new cannon and crew of new SPG were stationed inside more conveniently.
“Object 249” was designated ISU-122-2 or ISU-122S. All three self-propelled guns – ISU-152, ISU-122 and ISU-122S were produced using IS heavy tank chassis for easy production.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/usa/T19_HMG_afrika]
The T19 Howitzer Motor Carriage variant of the M3 halftrack was built by Diamond T in 1942. Only 324 were ever built. It was an expedient version mounting a 105mm howitzer in an almost standard M3 : the chassis was strengthened to take the extra loads, the windshield was hinged to fold forwards, a platform was built to take the gun and the fuel tanks were moved to the rear. 
In use it was found to be overloaded and very cramped. The armoured body offered little protection and there was no overhead cover. Despite these drawbacks the T19 allowed the US army to field a howitzer motor carriage in time for the North African campaign. It served as a stop-gap until the M7 Priest was ready. 
It was never standardized, that is it always retained its T19 designation and was never given a 'M' number. It saw active service in North Africa and Sicily and a few are believed to have soldiered on into Italy.

When the Priest appeared during 1942 there was no longer a need for the T19 and therefore those that had been shipped overseas and not destroyed in combat were eventually converted back to M3's.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/usa/m10_(wolverine)]
3-inch Gun Motor Carriage M10 Wolverine 76mm Anti-Tank Self-Propelled Gun

The history of self-propelled tank destroyers in the US is rather unusual. By 1940, the ongoing combat in Europe proved that tanks played a crucial part in modern warfare, and therefore highly mobile anti-tank guns were needed to counter the threat. At the same time, the attempts to realize the new vision encountered multiple problems with existing vehicle chassis and anti-tank guns. First attempts were less than successful. For example, the M6 Fargo Gun Motor Carriage was merely a 37mm M3 gun installed on top of a Dodge WC-52 weapons carrier jeep. Another tank destroyer, the M3 GMC, was an ancient M1897A4 75mm gun installed on the M3 Half-Track; another M3-based tank destroyer, the T48 with the M1 57mm gun, was exported to the UK, and eventually ended up in the Soviet Union. Both of these tank destroyer saw action in the Pacific and in Tunisia, but it quickly became clear that a more capable tank destroyer was needed, combining good off-road capabilities and adequate crew protection.

Work on a new tank destroyer design began in April of 1942, based on the M4 Sherman hull. Compared to the base M4A2 with a diesel engine, the hull ended up going through considerable changes, for example the sides becoming sloped in the process. After a short design phase, the new tank destroyer went into production in June of 1942.

The M10 had a rather novel design for its time, more resembling a full-fledged tank than a tank destroyer, both internally and externally. One of the peculiar features was a fully traversable open turret, housing a powerful 3-inch (76mm) M7 gun, originally an anti-aircraft weapon. Compared to the Sherman, the Wolverine’s armor was somewhat thinner, which was however remedied by having the armor sloped. Altogether, the M10 was a very successful design, combining a powerful gun, great maneuverability, and a comfortable and well-protected crew compartment. The Wolverine first saw action in Tunisia, where they were very much needed, being virtually the only armored vehicle in the American arsenal capable of engaging the Tiger tank. Subsequently, the M10 saw action in Italy, France and Germany. Many of the tank destroyers were sent to the UK, where they were equipped with a 17-pdr gun, and designated Achilles. 52 Wolverines were also sent to Russia, where two self-propelled artillery regiments were formed around them, that saw action in Byelorussia.

A total of 4,993 M10s and 1,731 M10A1s (based on the M4A3 Sherman, the last 300 of which were equipped with a M36 GMC turret) were produced between September 1942 and December 1943. After that, the M10 was supplanted by the M36 GMC, based on the M10 but equipped with the more powerful 90mm M3 gun.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/usa/M7_Priest]
The 105 mm Howitzer Motor Carriage M7 was an American self-propelled artillery vehicle produced during World War II. It was given the official service name 105 mm Self Propelled Gun, Priest by the British Army, due to the pulpit-like machine gun ring, and following on from the Bishop and the contemporary Deacon self-propelled guns.

During the early stages of World War II, US Army observers realized that they would need a self-propelled artillery vehicle with sufficient firepower to support armored operations. Lessons learned with half-tracks (such as the T19 Howitzer Motor Carriage (HMC) with a 105 mm howitzer on the M3 Half-track chassis) also showed that this vehicle would have to be armored and fully tracked. It was decided to use the M3 Lee chassis as the basis for this new vehicle design, named T32. The pilot vehicles used the M3 chassis with an open-topped superstructure, mounting an M2A1 105 mm howitzer, with a machine-gun added after trials.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/usa/m36_(slugger)]
Development of the 3 inch Gun Motor Carriage, equipped with a 90-mm anti-aircraft gun, began in summer 1942. Tests showed that the gun would fit the turret, but additional counterbalances were required to balance the long barrel. Taking all pros and cons into account, the designers started developing a tank destroyer with a reworked turret. The summer saw the production of two prototypes, different in chassis: the first vehicle was derived from the M10, and the second from the M10A1. The prototypes received the designations of T71E1 and T71 respectively. The 90-mm M3 gun was used as the main weapon, and a 12.7-mm Browning M2HB anti-aircraft machine-gun was also added. Just like with the M10, there was no coaxial machine-gun or bow-gun. The reworked turret featured a counterbalance for the gun. Mass-production of the T71E1 began in April 1943, and the tank destroyer was standardized and designated 90 mm Gun Motor Carriage M36 in July that year. The vehicle is also known as Jackson or Slugger. A total of 1,413 tank destroyers of that type were manufactured.
The 90 mm Gun Motor Carriage was the most powerful U.S. tank destroyer during World War II. A subcaliber shell fired from the 90-mm M3 gun was capable of piercing an armor plate as thick as 189 mm from a distance of one kilometer, enough to ensure the destruction of any German tank. The only type of German armor immune to the M36 was the Jagdtiger tank destroyer. Just like other U.S. tank destroyers, the M36 had relatively weak armor and no coaxial machine-gun or bow-gun. The latter factor considerably reduced its efficiency in fighting against enemy infantry.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/usa/M36B2]
90mm Gun Motor Carriage, M36B2

In October of 1942, US military command began work on an artillery piece based on the 90mm air defense gun, but which would be suitable for mounting onto SPGs. The validity of this directive was soon confirmed in Tunisia, where for the first time US forces encountered heavy German Tiger tanks. By the beginning of 1943, the gun had been completed, but it proved to be too long and heavy to mount on the turret of the M10 tank destroyer. For this reason, the development of a new turret was launched in March of the same year. Development was completed the following spring, and in June of 1944, the new SPG was adopted as the M36.
The first and most widespread variant of the M36 (of which 1525 units were made) was a new turret with a 90mm gun mounted on the chassis of the M10A1 tank destroyer. Some SPGs were simply constructed from existing M10A1 units. The second variant – the M36В1 (of which 187 units were made) – was mounted on the chassis of the M4A3 tank. The third and final variant – the M36В2 – was mounted on the M10 tank destroyer chassis. In April and May of 1945, 237 M10 vehicles were converted into M36В2s by the American Locomotive Company. Compared with other similar vehicles, the open-top M36В2 also had a shield to protect its crew from projectiles, hand grenades and inclement weather.
In terms of design, the M36 was a development of the M10 – a pure-blooded US tank destroyer with relatively weak armor and an open-top rotating turret. The gun of the M36 could easily deal with most German armored vehicles (especially when using its sub-caliber rounds), and so the SPG established a good reputation in the Army.
The M36В2 SPG left the construction line too late to face German forces, but being the newest tank destroyer of the US, it was used in the Korean War. Bt as the North Koreans did not make frequent use of tanks, the M36 SPG was used primarily against fieldworks.
During the postwar years, various M36 variants were exported to a number of countries, where they served for many years. According to reports, the most recent use of this SPG was in the Yugoslav Wars.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/uk/Bishop_afrika]
The rapid manoeuvre warfare practiced in the North African Campaign led to a requirement for a self-propelled artillery vehicle armed with the 25 pounder gun-howitzer. In June 1941 the development was entrusted to the Birmingham Railway Carriage and Wagon Company. A prototype was ready for trials by August and 100 were ordered by November 1941. The result was a vehicle designated Ordnance QF 25-pdr on Carrier Valentine 25-pdr Mk 1 and universally known as Bishop.

The Bishop was based on the Valentine II hull, with the turret replaced by a fixed boxy superstructure with large rear doors. Into this superstructure the 25 pounder gun-howitzer was fitted. As a consequence of the gun mounting the resulting vehicle had very high silhouette, a disadvantage in desert warfare. The maximum elevation for the gun was limited to 15 degrees, lowering the range considerably to about 6,400 yards (about half that of the gun on its wheeled carriage), the maximum depression was 5 degrees and traverse 8 degrees. In addition to the main armament the vehicle could carry a Bren light machine gun. By July 1942 80 Bishops had been built, and as the last 20 were being built an order for a further 50 was placed, with an option for a further 200, but the tender was abandoned in favour of the American M7.

The Bishop first saw action during the Second Battle of El Alamein in North Africa and remained in service during the early part of the Italian Campaign. Due to the aforementioned limitations, compounding the Valentine's characteristic slow speed, the Bishop was poorly received almost universally. In order to compensate for insufficient elevation, crews would often build large ramps out of the earth - running the Bishop onto these tilted the whole vehicle back effectively gaining extra elevation for the gun and increasing its maximum range.

It was replaced in service by the M7 Priest (105 mm) and Sexton (25-pounder) when those became available in sufficient numbers, and surviving Bishops were diverted for artillery use.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/UK/M10_Achilles]
17-pounder, Self Propelled, Achilles Mk.Ic

In 1943, the British army lend-leased from the U.S. their first M10 tank destroyers, which they designated "Wolverines." But although these destroyers were fine vehicles in their own right, they did not entirely satisfy the British military. Their current 76mm guns were non-standard for the British army, which led to issues with ammunition supply, which the U.S. provided along with the vehicles. In addition, while the gun’s ballistic characteristics surpassed those of every British tank gun of the time, the 17-pounder anti-tank gun was better still. For these reasons the decision was made to re-equip the leased M10s with the latter. The new tank destroyer was designated the "Achilles."

Aside from the new 17-pounder gun, which had to be counterweighted at the muzzle brake, the Achilles was very much like its prototype .

For a number of reasons, the Achilles conversion of the M10 was delayed until April 1944, so by the time of the invasion of Normandy, only 124 vehicles had been re-equipped. Thus, the British had little choice but use regular M10 tank destroyers as well. As a rule, a British anti-tank artillery regiment included two batteries towing 17-pounders, a battery of Achilles destroyers, and a battery of standard M10s.

The Achilles established a good reputation in combat, proving itself as one of the few reliable means of destroying well-armoured German vehicles, especially after subcalibre rounds were  added to its ammunition stores. Unlike the Americans, who freely used tank destroyers in active combat situations, the British treated them more strategically as highly mobile anti-tank guns, which reduced their losses to a certain extent.

Ultimately, a total of 1100 of the 1650 lend-leased British M10s were converted into Achilles. This included both the M10 and M10А1, the latter receiving the designation of Mk. IRC.

[EncDescription_data/units/spg/uk/archer]
SP 17pdr, Valentine, Mk I, Archer

The Archer antitank self-propelled gun was designed in 1942-1943 on the chassis of the Valentine infantry tank. The Vickers company was responsible for the development. The self-propelled gun carried a 17-pdr antitank gun with a barrel length of 58 calibers, capable of engaging nearly all types of German tanks.
The British forces used the Archer system in Europe from 1944 to the end of the war. Unlike the M10 and Achilles self-propelled guns, which were organic to armor divisions and brigades, Archer systems were in the inventory of infantry divisions and army tank brigades. 
After the landing in Normandy, subcaliber shells were made available for the 17-pdr gun. This allowed the Archer system to combat German tanks successfully. 
The vehicles remained in the British army’s inventory until the early 1950s. 

[EncDescription_data/units/SPG/Ita/Semovente_DA_75_18_M40]
Semovente da 75/18 (Scafo M40) - assault gun on M13/40 tank chassis

The successful design of the German StuG III assault guns prompted the Italian military to the idea of creating its analogue, especially since both in terms of armament and armor, Italian tanks for direct infantry support in the presence of enemy defense saturated with anti-tank guns were poorly suited. As a base for the self-propelled gun, a light (according to the Italian classification - medium) M13/40 tank was chosen, on the chassis of which an armored wheelhouse with a 75-mm howitzer with a long barrel of 18 calibers equipped with a muzzle brake was installed.
The prototype of the machine was successfully tested in early 1941, in the same year the serial production of new self-propelled guns began.

Semovente da 75/18 became one of the most successful examples of Italian armored vehicles. The gun of the machine coped well with the defeat of enemy firing points, and the presence of a cumulative projectile in the ammunition allowed it to successfully fight almost all the armored vehicles of the allies. The installation of the gun provided a record horizontal guidance angle of almost 40 degrees, which largely offset the main drawback of most self-propelled guns - the lack of a rotating turret. The disadvantages of the car were low speed, as well as weak booking of the side. The frontal armor of the conning tower protected well from small-caliber shells, but it was ineffective against 57-mm and 75-mm shells.

During 1940, 60 Semovente da 75/18 self-propelled guns were manufactured on the basis of the M13/40 tank, after which a self-propelled gun not much different from it on the chassis of the M14/41 tank went into production. Manufactured self-propelled guns have been used in North Africa since the beginning of 1942.



[EncDescription_data/units/SPG/Ita/Semovente_DA_75_34_M41]
Semovente da 75/34 (Scafo M41) - assault gun on M14/41 tank chassis

The short-barreled howitzer, which was armed with Italian Semovente assault guns, had a low muzzle velocity, which reduced the range of a direct shot and the effectiveness of firing an armor-piercing projectile. These factors led to the creation of Semovente, armed with a more powerful 75 mm cannon with a barrel length of 34 caliber. The new gun significantly increased the capabilities of self-propelled guns to combat armored vehicles, but the reservation of self-propelled guns remained weak.

Most self-propelled guns with long-barreled 75 mm guns were built on the chassis of M15/42 tanks, also 25 Semovente da 75/18 self-propelled guns (Scafo M41) were converted for the installation of this gun.